The outline contains no objective, work strand or impact claim involving the design, engineering or construction of a flood wall or any other permanent barrier. Any of the following observational or modelling alternatives is compliant:
- temporary monitoring campaigns on outfall blocking, floodwater and runoff pathways, for example with pressure loggers, automatic samplers, drifters or ADCP transects;
- modelling of exposure under existing defences, with outfall closure used as a boundary condition;
- co-designed warning indicators delivered to the municipality and the water board.

Existing barriers may appear as context or as model inputs, but the project produces no barrier designs.

The outline contains no objective, work strand or impact claim involving the design, engineering or construction of a flood wall or any other permanent barrier. Any of the following observational or modelling alternatives is compliant:
- temporary monitoring campaigns, such as pressure loggers, automatic samplers, drifters or ADCP transects, on outfall blocking, floodwater and runoff pathways;
- modelling of exposure under existing defences and outfall closure used as boundary conditions;
- co-designed warning indicators delivered to the municipality and water board.
Existing barriers may appear as context or as model inputs, but the project produces no barrier designs.

In Excellence, the objectives and methodology centre on a waterborne exposure pathway:
- catchment runoff, modelled with WEIH's rainfall-runoff models;
- estuarine transport, modelled with TELEMAC-2D or the 1D network model;
- coastal or urban exposure at the end of the chain.

The methodology does three things:
- states hypotheses on estuarine transport;
- describes temporary sampling campaigns that use FBMI's field equipment and its accredited faecal-indicator laboratory;
- quantifies uncertainty along the chain up to the early-warning thresholds.

ZUT's planning methods (block-level exposure, distributional analysis, co-design) link the modelled pathways to who is exposed. They are also used to co-design warning indicators with authorities.

Housing-layout optimisation is not an objective. It is either absent or a minor downstream illustration.

The Impact pathway is framed around early-warning indicators for coastal authorities and communities, not around housing layouts.

The case area is open, provided the full pathway is covered. Any of these is compliant:
- the Dordrecht tidal reaches;
- an Ems or Weser estuary with its coast;
- a Danish Wadden Sea system.

